# Real credentials never get committed — .env and any variant of it.
#
# The negation is load-bearing. `.env*` on its own also matched `.env.example`,
# which silently kept the template out of the repository: `git status` stayed
# clean, nothing ever warned, and a fresh clone had no `.env.example` for
# `copy .env.example .env` to find. That template is the only enumeration of
# which variables exist, so losing it costs far more than it looks.
#
# It is safe to commit because every credential slot in it is empty — the only
# values it carries are ports, booleans, model names and localhost URLs.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Rendered policy documents and the operator's answers to their placeholders.
# Generated by `python scripts/legal.py` and specific to one deployment, so the
# templates (PRIVACY.md, TERMS.md) are what lives in the repo, not the output.
legal/
